"""
Monotonic instants and elapsed-time operations.

An `Instant` belongs to the selected runtime provider's monotonic domain. Its
numeric origin is private to the provider and must not be interpreted as a
wall-clock or calendar timestamp. Instants are copy-readable views for
comparison; they do not own an operating-system handle or allocated storage.
"""
"""
An immutable monotonic point captured from the active provider.

The stored tick value is provider-domain data. Applications should use the
time operations below rather than treating it as an epoch timestamp.
"""
open struct Instant {
    """The owned provider-domain tick used for same-domain ordering and subtraction."""
    erg ticks: u64,
}

"""
Capture the current instant from the active monotonic provider.

Returns a typed `Instant` with no allocation and no wall-clock semantics. The
selected runtime must provide a monotonic clock for this operation to exist.
"""
open verb now() -> Instant {
    return Instant {ticks: monotonic_nanos()};
}

"""
Read an instant's provider-domain tick value.

`instant` is borrowed as `abs` and remains owned by the caller. The returned
`u64` is meaningful only when compared with an instant from the same provider
domain; it is not a Unix timestamp.
"""
open verb instant_ticks(abs instant: Instant) -> u64 {
    return instant.ticks;
}

"""
Calculate the non-negative span from `earlier` to `later`.

Both instants are borrowed and must belong to the same monotonic provider
domain. Returns `Ok(Duration)` on valid ordering or
`Err(TimeError.NegativeDuration)` when `later` precedes `earlier`; no unsigned
underflow, wrapping, or allocation is permitted.
"""
open verb duration_since(abs later: Instant, abs earlier: Instant) -> Result[Duration, TimeError] {
    if later.ticks < earlier.ticks {
        return Result[Duration, TimeError].Err(TimeError.NegativeDuration);
    }
    erg elapsed_nanos: u64 = later.ticks - earlier.ticks;
    return Result[Duration, TimeError].Ok(duration_nanos(nanos: erg elapsed_nanos));
}

"""
Calculate elapsed time from an earlier instant to the current instant.

The earlier instant is borrowed and remains owned by the caller. The result is
typed and uses the same provider domain as `now()`; ordering failure is
reported as `TimeError.NegativeDuration`.
"""
open verb elapsed_since(abs earlier: Instant) -> Result[Duration, TimeError] {
    erg current: Instant = now();
    return duration_since(later: abs current, earlier: abs earlier);
}
